IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pild-dates`
IMPORT `pild-claimant`

-- Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act 2003 (NT) -- cases for the claimant's seat.
-- Every expected value is worked by hand from the deposited text (reprint REPP078), section by section, never copied from
-- the encoding's output. Edges: the commencement days (1 May 2003, 15 December 2010, 3 June 2022) and the day before;
-- 15 and 16 years of age (s 15); the 25% floor (s 17); 17 and 18 years of age (s 3); the 120th and 121st day (s 17K);
-- the 28th and 29th day (ss 17K(3), 32J(5)); 3 times average weekly earnings and just over (s 20); 6 hours and 6 months
-- and just under, 40 hours (s 23); every step of the s 27 Table and the gaps between steps; the last days of February and
-- August and the first of March and September (s 30); every band of s 32E and the 15 000 unit cap; 12 months from 31
-- March and from 29 February (s 32H).
--
-- The regulative rules are asserted through CONSIDER EVALTRACE ... WHEN FULFILLED (a residual DEONTIC cannot be compared
-- with EQUALS on this l4), and one breach of each is #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line.

`any day` MEANS `the day` 1 1 2025

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 4: does the Act apply. HYPOTHETICAL: Ana slips on a wet floor in a Darwin supermarket and sues in negligence.
`damages claim` MEANS `a claim for damages for a personal injury (for example in negligence, or for breach of contract or of a statutory duty)`
`ordinary injury` MEANS `some other injury (including a psychological injury, a disease not caused by dust, or a death)`
`the Act applies` MEANS `yes: the Act applies to my claim (s 4(1)); Part 4A applies only to claims by a person in custody for assault, battery or false imprisonment`
`before commencement` MEANS `mostly no: the incident happened before the Act commenced on 1 May 2003 (s 4(2)); only Part 4, Division 6 applies, and for a child abuse claim Part 3A Divisions 1, 4, 5 and 6 (s 17B(5))`
`dust excluded` MEANS `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(c) (a dust-related condition); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
#ASSERT `Does this Act apply question` `damages claim` `ordinary injury` (`the day` 14 3 2025) EQUALS `the Act applies`
-- The commencement day itself, and the day before.
#ASSERT `Does this Act apply question` `damages claim` `ordinary injury` (`the day` 1 5 2003) EQUALS `the Act applies`
#ASSERT `Does this Act apply question` `damages claim` `ordinary injury` (`the day` 30 4 2003) EQUALS `before commencement`
-- Mesothelioma, and a dust condition not named in s 4(5)(a).
#ASSERT `Does this Act apply question` `damages claim` `a disease named in section 4(5)(a) (aluminosis, asbestosis, asbestos induced carcinoma, asbestos related pleural disease, bagassosis, berylliosis, byssinosis, coal dust pneumoconiosis, farmer's lung, hard metal pneumoconiosis, mesothelioma, silicosis, silico-tuberculosis or talcosis)` (`the day` 14 3 2025) EQUALS `dust excluded`
#ASSERT `Does this Act apply question` `damages claim` `another condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust` (`the day` 14 3 2025) EQUALS `dust excluded`
#ASSERT `Does this Act apply question` `a claim for benefits for a death or injury from a motor accident under the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979` `ordinary injury` (`the day` 14 3 2025) EQUALS `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(a) (motor accident benefits); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
#ASSERT `Does this Act apply question` `a claim for compensation under the Return to Work Act 1986` `ordinary injury` (`the day` 14 3 2025) EQUALS `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(b) (workers compensation); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
#ASSERT `Does this Act apply question` `a claim about goods under section 106, 107, 118, 127 or 136 of the Australian Consumer Law, for a personal injury` `ordinary injury` (`the day` 14 3 2025) EQUALS `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(e) (Australian Consumer Law goods claim); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`

-- s 5: a death claim under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974.
`Part 4 applies to the death` MEANS `yes: Part 4 (the limits on damages) applies to the assessment, in addition to section 10 of the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (s 5(2))`
`Part 4 does not apply to the death` MEANS `no: section 5(2) does not apply Part 4 to this death; this Act does not limit the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (s 5(1))`
#ASSERT `Does Part 4 apply to a death claim question` (`the day` 1 5 2003) TRUE EQUALS `Part 4 applies to the death`
#ASSERT `Does Part 4 apply to a death claim question` (`the day` 30 4 2003) TRUE EQUALS `Part 4 does not apply to the death`
#ASSERT `Does Part 4 apply to a death claim question` (`the day` 1 5 2020) FALSE EQUALS `Part 4 does not apply to the death`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 7: a volunteer. HYPOTHETICAL: Ben is knocked over by a volunteer marshal at a church fete.
`religious` MEANS `a religious body`
`listed purpose` MEANS `a purpose listed in section 7(7)(a)(i) to (vii) (religious, educational, charitable, benevolent, literature, science, the arts, sport, recreation, amusement, the environment, a community, social or cultural centre, a local community's interests, or a political purpose)`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `religious` `listed purpose` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `the volunteer is not personally liable; the organisation is liable as if the volunteer were its employee, and members of its management committee are not personally liable (s 7(1), (3), (6))`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `a body corporate (for example an incorporated association or a company)` `listed purpose` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `the volunteer is not personally liable; the organisation is liable as if the volunteer were its employee, and members of its management committee are not personally liable (s 7(1), (3), (6))`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `an Agency or department of the Territory` `listed purpose` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `the volunteer is not personally liable; the Territory is liable as if the volunteer were the Agency's employee (s 7(1), (3), (4))`
-- An unincorporated club is not a "community organisation" (finding 1).
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `some other body (for example an unincorporated club or group)` `listed purpose` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: the organisation is not a community organisation (a religious body, a body corporate, or a Territory Agency or department) (s 7(7))`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `religious` `listed purpose` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: work done under a community work or correction order is not community work (s 7(7))`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `religious` `some other purpose` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `"community work" includes work for a purpose prescribed by regulation (s 7(7)(a)(viii)); the Regulations are not deposited`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `religious` `listed purpose` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `"volunteer" includes a person paid no more than an amount prescribed by the Regulations (s 7(7)(b)); the Regulations are not deposited`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `religious` `listed purpose` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: the act was not done in good faith and without recklessness (s 7(1))`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `religious` `listed purpose` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: they knew or ought reasonably to have known they were acting outside their authority or against the organisation's instructions (s 7(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Who is liable for the volunteer question` `religious` `listed purpose` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: they did the act while intoxicated (s 7(2)(b))`

-- s 7A: donated food. HYPOTHETICAL: Cleo eats a donated meal from a food bank.
`donor protected` MEANS `answered` `the donor is not liable for the injury (s 7A(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `food` (`the day` 15 12 2010) FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `donor protected`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `food` (`the day` 14 12 2010) FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the donation was made before section 7A commenced on 15 December 2010 (s 36)`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `food` `any day` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: a person who only distributes food or products donated by someone else is not a donor (s 7A(4))`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `some other product` `any day` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `"grocery product" includes a product prescribed by regulation (s 7A(4)(d)); the Regulations are not deposited`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `a household cleaning product` `any day` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `donor protected`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `food` `any day` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the donation was not in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, free to the consumer (s 7A(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `food` `any day` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the food was not fit to eat, or the product not safe to use, when it left the donor (s 7A(2)(b))`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `food` `any day` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the donor did not pass on the handling requirements (s 7A(2)(c))`
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `food` `any day` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the donor did not pass on the time limit (s 7A(2)(d))`
-- Nothing needed handling and nothing had a time limit: nothing to pass on.
#ASSERT `Is the donor liable question` `food` `any day` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `donor protected`

-- s 8: good Samaritans.
`helped` MEANS `came to my aid in an emergency`
`advised` MEANS `gave advice about my treatment in a medical emergency, as a person with medical qualifications (a doctor, a health professional recognised by statute, or an ambulance officer or paramedic)`
#ASSERT `Is the good Samaritan liable question` `helped` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the helper is not personally liable for an act done while giving me emergency assistance (s 8(1))`
#ASSERT `Is the good Samaritan liable question` `advised` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the helper is not personally liable for the advice about my treatment (s 8(2))`
#ASSERT `Is the good Samaritan liable question` `helped` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `section 8 gives no protection: the helper expected payment or other consideration, so is not a good Samaritan (s 8(4))`
#ASSERT `Is the good Samaritan liable question` `helped` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `section 8 gives no protection: I was not apparently in need of emergency assistance, so the helper is not a good Samaritan (s 8(4))`
#ASSERT `Is the good Samaritan liable question` `helped` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `section 8 gives no protection: the helper did not act in good faith and without recklessness (s 8(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Is the good Samaritan liable question` `advised` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `section 8 gives no protection: the helper was intoxicated (s 8(3))`

-- s 9: the burglar who falls through a skylight.
#ASSERT `Can I claim against the occupier question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the occupier or owner is not liable for an injury to a person entering premises who intends to commit, is committing or has committed an imprisonable offence there (s 9(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I claim against the occupier question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `section 9 does not stand in the way of my claim`
#ASSERT `Can I claim against the occupier question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `section 9 does not stand in the way of my claim`

-- s 10: injured while offending.
#ASSERT `Does my own offence bar my claim question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: no one is liable for my injury, because I was hurt while committing an imprisonable offence that contributed materially to the risk (s 10(1))`
#ASSERT `Does my own offence bar my claim question` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: no one is liable for my injury, because I was hurt while committing an imprisonable offence that contributed materially to the risk (s 10(1))`
#ASSERT `Does my own offence bar my claim question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the court is satisfied the case is exceptional and excluding liability would be harsh and unjust, so section 10(1) does not apply (s 10(2))`
#ASSERT `Does my own offence bar my claim question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: section 10 does not stand in the way of my claim`

-- ss 12, 13: apologies.
#ASSERT `Can I use the apology question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: an expression of regret made before the proceeding began is not admissible as evidence in it (s 13)`
#ASSERT `Can I use the apology question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `section 13 does not keep it out: the statement acknowledged fault, so it is not an expression of regret (s 12(b)); whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`
#ASSERT `Can I use the apology question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `section 13 does not keep it out: it was made after the proceeding began; whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`
#ASSERT `Can I use the apology question` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `section 13 does not keep it out: the statement did not express regret for the incident (s 12(a)); whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 14-17: contributory negligence. HYPOTHETICAL: Dev, drunk, walks onto a road; Eli, 16, rides with a drunk driver.
`presumed` MEANS `yes: contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted, so it is established and my damages are reduced by at least 25% (ss 14, 15, 17)`
`rebutted` MEANS `a presumption arises but I rebut it, so Part 3 does not establish contributory negligence (ss 14(2), 15(2)); the ordinary law of contributory negligence still applies`
`none arises` MEANS `no presumption: neither section 14 nor section 15 applies on these facts`
-- Dev: intoxicated, not rebutted.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `presumed`
-- Dev: not alleged.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no presumption: the respondent does not allege contributory negligence (ss 14(1)(b), 15(1)(c))`
-- Dev: a blood reading of 0.08 found by the court is conclusive (s 16), even if he says he was not affected.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `presumed`
-- Dev: his drink was spiked (involuntary).
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `rebutted`
-- Dev: sober.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `none arises`
-- Eli at 16, and at 15.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 16 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `presumed`
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 15 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `none arises`
-- Eli: the driver's reading of 0.08 or more makes the driver intoxicated (s 16).
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 16 TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `presumed`
-- Eli: not aware and ought not to have been.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 16 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `none arises`
-- Eli: the injury was not caused by the driver's negligence.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 16 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `none arises`
-- Eli rebuts.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 16 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `rebutted`
-- Both drunk: Eli rebuts the s 15 presumption but not his own s 14 one.
#ASSERT `Is contributory negligence presumed question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE 16 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `presumed`

-- s 17: at least 25%.
#ASSERT `My damages after the section 17 reduction question` 100000 20 EQUALS 75000
#ASSERT `My damages after the section 17 reduction question` 100000 25 EQUALS 75000
#ASSERT `My damages after the section 17 reduction question` 100000 30 EQUALS 70000
#ASSERT `My damages after the section 17 reduction question` 100000 100 EQUALS 0

